<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis de Bourbon (bishop of Liège)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1438">1438</a>–<a href="/years/1482">1482</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> was prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Liège from 1456 to 1482, imposed by his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. His rule provoked repeated risings, which ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a>'s sack of Liège in 1468 and a Burgundian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. He was killed in 1482 by followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-de-la-marck">William de la Marck</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-de-bourbon-bishop-of-liege.jpg" alt="Louis de Bourbon (bishop of Liège)" width="301" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SirachMatthews · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_de_Bourbon(Couleurs).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bishops and towns struggled over taxation and jurisdiction for centuries, and outside powers intervened: the dukes of Burgundy imposed <b>Louis de Bourbon</b> as bishop in 1456, and from 1581 to 1688 members of the Bavarian house of Wittelsbach, who were also archbishops of Cologne, held the see.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
